WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.320
Okay.

00:04.360 --> 00:06.040
So now what is a filtered index?

00:06.080 --> 00:09.960
A filtered index is a regular index, but with a twist.

00:09.960 --> 00:13.800
It only includes rows that meet specific condition.

00:13.960 --> 00:15.600
So let's understand what this means.

00:15.640 --> 00:19.640
So again we have our non-clustered index and the B3 structure.

00:19.720 --> 00:21.400
So now add the leaf nodes.

00:21.400 --> 00:26.560
We will get only the IDs the data that fulfill a specific condition.

00:26.600 --> 00:31.480
So for example if you are saying we want only the active customers this is the condition.

00:31.480 --> 00:37.200
So that means on the leaf nodes we will have only the customer IDs that are active.

00:37.200 --> 00:43.600
And any inactive customer will not be included at all at the database and at the nodes.

00:43.600 --> 00:49.280
So that means our B structure is going to be a little bit smaller as usual, because we have less data

00:49.280 --> 00:51.760
included in the structure.

00:51.760 --> 00:55.800
So our index is going to be smaller than the regular non-clustered index.

00:55.800 --> 00:59.840
So now the question is why is it important to have a filtered index.

00:59.920 --> 01:04.240
Well the biggest benefit is we're going to have targeted optimizations.

01:04.240 --> 01:12.040
So for example if our analysis always focuses on the active users and inactive users are totally irrelevant.

01:12.040 --> 01:18.960
So that means having only relevant subset of data in their index going to make the whole index much

01:18.960 --> 01:22.400
smaller, which leads to faster performance.

01:22.400 --> 01:27.440
So it's going to be faster to query this filtered B-tree structure.

01:27.440 --> 01:33.040
So that means we are doing targeted optimizations and we are improving the query performance.

01:33.240 --> 01:38.720
Now the second benefit if you think about the storage, since the size of the structure is going to

01:38.720 --> 01:44.760
be smaller, that means we're going to need less storage space in order to store the index, which is

01:44.760 --> 01:48.280
a great thing if you have large tables in your database.

01:48.280 --> 01:54.280
So the filtered index is going to make the structure of the index smaller, which can improve the speed

01:54.280 --> 01:59.160
and the performance, and as well reduce the storage that is needed for your index.

01:59.200 --> 01:59.440
Okay.

01:59.480 --> 02:02.000
So now let's check the syntax of the filtered index.

02:02.000 --> 02:02.760
It's very simple.

02:02.760 --> 02:10.160
It's like any query you can go and add at the end of creating the index the where clause and then the

02:10.160 --> 02:13.000
condition as you are doing in any Select statement.

02:13.000 --> 02:16.870
But the SQL server is very restrictive using this type of index.

02:17.110 --> 02:21.750
So you cannot use filtered index on a clustered index.

02:21.750 --> 02:26.950
So it is only allowed for the non-clustered index because it makes no sense.

02:26.950 --> 02:32.950
If you create a clustered index, the entire table should be reorganized and ordered.

02:32.950 --> 02:35.670
So it will not work for only subset of data.

02:35.710 --> 02:39.990
And as well you cannot create a filtered index on a column store.

02:40.190 --> 02:43.630
So it is only allowed if you are using row store.

02:43.670 --> 02:47.710
But you can go and combine the unique index together with the filtered index.

02:47.990 --> 02:50.590
There is no restrictions so it can be like this.

02:50.630 --> 02:56.390
Create unique non-clustered index on the table and then you specify the where condition.

02:56.390 --> 02:58.510
So this is the syntax of the filtered index.

02:58.510 --> 03:00.390
And we have these restrictions.

03:00.430 --> 03:00.830
All right.

03:00.830 --> 03:05.070
So now let's say that we have the following query where we are selecting data from customers.

03:05.070 --> 03:11.790
But always in our program or in our report we are selecting only the customers from USA.

03:11.990 --> 03:13.430
So we have the following condition.

03:13.470 --> 03:18.710
It says where country equal to USA and execute.

03:18.710 --> 03:22.430
So this is the basics of many queries that we have in our project.

03:22.430 --> 03:26.150
And we are always filtering the customers based on their country.

03:26.150 --> 03:31.950
So in one query, we are finding maybe the top customers and the other query we are finding the average

03:31.950 --> 03:33.190
of scores and so on.

03:33.190 --> 03:35.510
But we are always filtering the data like this.

03:35.550 --> 03:37.510
Where country equal to USA.

03:37.550 --> 03:42.990
So now since we are using this column lot and our table may be getting like millions of records, we

03:42.990 --> 03:46.350
can go and create non-clustered index on this column.

03:46.350 --> 03:55.710
So the usual way we go over here and say create non-clustered index and we call it like this index customers

03:56.350 --> 03:57.310
country.

03:57.510 --> 04:02.110
And then it's going to be on the table sales customers.

04:02.350 --> 04:05.630
And we select the column country like this.

04:05.630 --> 04:12.310
So if you do it like this it's going to go and create a non-clustered index for all customers not only

04:12.350 --> 04:14.790
from USA but for everything.

04:14.790 --> 04:19.830
So even if the customers come from Germany, which is not really necessary because in our project we

04:19.830 --> 04:22.550
only focus on the customers from USA.

04:22.670 --> 04:28.750
So instead of that we can go and include the where condition inside our cluster.

04:28.750 --> 04:29.950
So it's very simple.

04:29.950 --> 04:34.580
We're going to go and say where country equal to USA.

04:34.620 --> 04:36.260
Exactly like our query.

04:36.260 --> 04:38.540
So now the index that's going to be built.

04:38.540 --> 04:45.860
It will be focused and targeted only for subset of data, only for the data that fulfill this condition.

04:45.940 --> 04:49.860
So now let's go and create our filtered index and it is working.

04:49.860 --> 04:53.020
Let's go and check our indexes on the customers.

04:53.220 --> 04:56.220
So let's go to the indexes over here and refresh.

04:56.220 --> 04:57.740
Now we can see our index over here.

04:57.740 --> 05:02.060
It says it is not unique because we didn't find anything at the start.

05:02.060 --> 05:05.500
So duplicates are allowed of course which is what we defined here.

05:05.500 --> 05:07.500
And as well it is filtered.

05:07.500 --> 05:10.260
So it doesn't contain all the rows from your table.

05:10.260 --> 05:14.140
It contains only the rows that fulfill our condition.

05:14.140 --> 05:21.140
So that means now if I go and execute this query, the index can be used because the rows of this query

05:21.140 --> 05:23.140
is included in the index.

05:23.140 --> 05:29.620
But if I go over here and say Germany and execute the query, it's going to be slower because all those

05:29.620 --> 05:33.860
rows inside the query is not part of our index.

05:33.860 --> 05:37.900
So this index will not be used at all in order to improve the query.

05:37.900 --> 05:41.260
So this is how we work with the filtered index in SQL.
